        "reported_material_gaps": [],
        "research_rationale": "Identity: the registered original is the Federal Register printing of Executive Order 10222 (16 FR 2247; govinfo PDF text layer, grade original_representation_unverified), headed Executive Order 10222, Providing for Certain Transfers to the Federal Civil Defense Administration, signed March 8, 1951. Inventory: three numbered paragraphs, confirmed as three clauses: 1, transferring the civil-defense part of the health services and special weapons defense functions of the Health Resources Office of the National Security Resources Board to the Federal Civil Defense Administration, to remain under the Administrator's jurisdiction until further order of the President; 2, transferring related property, records and personnel; 3, effective as of March 4, 1951. Ending: section 904(a)(2) of Executive Order 12919 of June 3, 1994 (59 FR 29525, held government printing) provides that Executive Order No. 10222 of March 8, 1951 (16 FR 2247) is superseded or revoked; the U.S. Code note at 42 U.S.C. 5195 note says the same and gives the date June 3, 1994. Section 904 has no stated effective date, so the end is read as effective on signing, 1994-06-03. Unnamed earlier candidates, disclosed under the unnamed-instruments ruling of 2026-10-21 (they do not name Executive Order 10222 and are not ends): Reorganization Plan No. 1 of 1958 (the editorial note in the held OFR 1989 Codification says it terminated the Federal Civil Defense Administration effective 1958-07-01 and consolidated its functions into the Office of Defense and Civilian Mobilization) and Executive Order 10952 of 1961-07-20 (the same note says civil defense functions were later transferred to the Secretary of Defense). The same 1989 Codification still prints Executive Order 10222 as in force on 1989-01-20, after both events. The plan's own text and Executive Order 10952 do not appear in the held corpus as acting on this order. Searches: the OFR executive-order catalog search and the USC and eCFR citation searches were selected after reading their coverage notes (complete; no coverage warning; the USC edition runs through 2026-10-09 and the eCFR search to 2026-10-14, which cannot matter for an order whose end is shown in 1986 or earlier).\nMember checks: {\"earlier_end_check\":\"Candidate earlier ends examined. (1) Reorganization Plan No. 1 of 1958, which terminated the Federal Civil Defense Administration, and Executive Order 10952 of 1961, which moved civil defense functions to the Secretary of Defense: neither names Executive Order 10222 in any held text, and no held source shows the order's commands extinguished by them; the 1989 Codification still printed the order as in force. Under the owner ruling of 2026-10-21 on unnamed instruments, a statute or order that does not name the target does not end it; the end is the instrument that names it. They are disclosed as unnamed earlier candidates. (2) The phrase until further order of the President in clause 1 is an own-terms contingent end; the only held presidential order ending it is Executive Order 12919. (3) The one-time transfers in clause 2 were presumably carried out in 1951 but no held source dates their performance, so no exhaustion date is asserted and the express supersession is the first held end. No earlier end is shown, so none is claimed.\",\"independent_command_check\":\"Independent commands: clause 1 (jurisdiction of the transferred functions under the Administrator until further order of the President) and clause 2 (transfer of property, records and personnel) are distinct commands of the same order; clause 3 fixes the effective date. None is shown in held evidence to have a separate source, statute or earlier sunset; each shares the express supersession by Executive Order 12919, with the executed transfers saved as accrued effects.\",\"savings_check\":\"Savings and residual effects: accrued effects of executed transfers (state transferred_or_accrued) with the Executive Order 12919 section 903 saving language as source.\",\"scope_check\":\"Scope: section 904(a)(2) of Executive Order 12919 names Executive Order No. 10222 of March 8, 1951 (16 FR 2247) without exception, so it reaches all three clauses. The dossier note that the 1989 current-law lead lacks an original-source sha256 is resolved by not using that editorial snapshot as a predicate: this is a NO order, and the lead is cited only as an editorial observation that the order was printed as in force on 1989-01-20 before its 1994 supersession.\"}",
